# `trap` in an interactive login bash on Ubuntu 22.04. Setting, listing (`trap -- 'CMD' SIGNAME` in
# signal order, `EXIT` first, bash's `DEBUG`, `ERR` and `RETURN` last), ignoring with an empty
# command, resetting with `-` or a lone signal, the refusals (`invalid signal specification`,
# `invalid option` with a usage line), signal names in any case with or without `SIG`, `RTMIN+N`,
# the numbers 32 and 33, `trap -l`, a subshell that lists its parent's handlers until it sets one,
# the `EXIT` handler (which runs when a subshell or a command substitution ends) and the `DEBUG`
# and `ERR` handlers (`DEBUG` before every simple command, `ERR` after a failing simple command,
# pipeline or subshell that nothing tests). `$BASH_COMMAND` is not modeled, so none of the
# handlers here read it. Each reply was produced by `bash --noprofile --norc --noediting -i`
# started as a login shell (`exec -a -bash`) in the localhost/propolis-survey-ref:jammy container
# (bash 5.1.16), run with `--network none`, PS1 and PS2 set to markers and these lines fed one at a
# time on standard input.
# source: ubuntu:22.04 container run of bash 5.1.16 (synthetic command shapes)
# date: 2026-10-10
# protocol: ssh
# persona: ubuntu
